Homer repeatedly turns material things into part of this causal machinery. Grethlein shows how the bed, bow, and orchard are not merely symbols but objects whose histories later become active in the plot: the bed can authenticate Odysseus as husband, the bow as hero, the orchard as heir \citep[pp.~467--82]{Grethlein2020Bed}. Currie similarly distinguishes knowing Odysseus's identity from acknowledging it and from restoring the relationship; recognition in Book 23 unfolds in stages rather than as a single proof event \citep[pp.~1--29]{Currie2022}.

The point is broader than recognition. Consequences in the second half of the poem are often designed by arranging who can see, know, possess, or do what. Intelligence is partly the capacity to alter another agent's future action space.

That creates a fresh ethical tension. Odysseus has learned that consequences exceed intention. He has also become extremely good at engineering consequences for people who know less than he does.

\section{The suitors: causal responsibility does not determine punishment}

When the killing begins in Book 22, Eurymachus offers Odysseus a rival causal account. Antinous, he says, was responsible for the whole enterprise; Antinous wanted the kingship and drove the others. Antinous is already dead. The survivors will repay the household for what they consumed (Od. 22.45--59).

The plea is self-interested, but that does not make its structure trivial. Eurymachus concedes wrongdoing while disputing how responsibility should be distributed and what repayment should count as sufficient. Odysseus rejects both the causal partition and the proposed remedy.

The distinction is crucial. Establishing that someone contributed to a wrong does not tell us the magnitude of punishment that should follow. Causal responsibility and proportionality are separate problems.

Loney's account of \term{tisis} makes this visible. \term{Tisis} is not neutral ``consequence'' but negative reciprocity, a repayment whose meaning depends on a narrative: an earlier offense, a victim, an avenger, a debt, a retaliatory act, and some judgment that the act counts as repayment rather than a fresh crime \citep{Loney2019}. The same physical killing can therefore occupy rival moral narratives.

This is where Bakker's analysis of meat and consumption adds material force. The companions consume Helios's cattle; the suitors consume Odysseus's household. In both cases unauthorized consumption generates a structure of repayment, and the poem deliberately aligns the wanderings with Ithaca through eating, scarcity, possession, and vengeance \citep{Bakker2013}. Moral causation in the \Od{} is often not abstract. Someone has eaten what belonged to someone else.

But even an intelligible debt needs a stopping rule. How much counts as enough?

\section{Book 24: the consequence becomes a cause}

The answer cannot come from causation alone. Book 24 proves it.

Odysseus has killed the suitors because of what they did. Their fathers and brothers now possess a new fact: Odysseus killed their sons and brothers. The massacre that counted as \term{tisis} from one perspective becomes a fresh offense from another. Eupeithes can mobilize a new revenge around a true remembered loss.

Every consequence can become the antecedent of the next consequence.

This is why Loney gives such weight to the ending. The poem's revenge logic does not close itself. Zeus and Athena must impose closure, coupling oaths and renewed peace with \term{eklesis}, a form of forgetting or letting-go of the killings (Od. 24.482--86). Loney describes the ending as requiring radical divine amnesia because the relatives' counter-revenge reveals the open-endedness of \term{tisis} \citep[pp.~1--10]{Loney2019Intro}.

The poem has arrived at an uncomfortable result. At its beginning, Zeus insists that suffering has antecedents and that mortals should stop attributing everything to the gods. At its end, remembering antecedents too faithfully threatens to perpetuate violence forever.

Correct causal memory is not yet peace.

A remembered harm can be true and still need to lose its power to authorize the next act. ``This happened'' and ``you may avenge it'' are different propositions. Book 24 forces them apart.

That distinction returns us to Nolan. Athena asks Odysseus what he sees when the men die. Seeing consequence is only the beginning. A causal interpretation becomes ethically dangerous when it silently acquires an imperative: therefore I must blame myself forever; therefore they deserve death; therefore their kin must retaliate; therefore the chain must continue.

\section{Responsibility without sovereignty}

Nolan makes Homer's distributed causal problem psychologically explicit. Odysseus looks at the dead and sees himself.

This is not entirely wrong. He chose routes. He withheld information at Scylla. He ignored his men at the Cyclops. He led, persuaded, commanded, gambled, and survived. A reading that turns every disaster into ``chance'' would be an evasion.

But the opposite reading is also an evasion. If every death is \emph{my failure}, Odysseus remains the sovereign center even in remorse. Total guilt can preserve the same fantasy of authorship as heroic pride: the world would have obeyed me if only I had been better.

Williams's language of centres of agency is useful here because it lets us say two things at once. Human beings are genuine sources of action, and their actions occur in worlds shaped by forces they did not originate \citep{Williams2008}. Odysseus does not need to be the ultimate cause of the storm, the war, the hunger, the crew's deliberation, or the gods in order to be responsible for what he actually does within those conditions.

That gives a more exact meaning to Athena's ``who led you?'' It is not a theological excuse. It is an attack on causal narcissism.

The scene's final question makes the stakes visible. Athena moves from ``why do you claim responsibility?'' to ``why don't you want to go home?'' The film links Odysseus's causal story to the identity he is maintaining. If he remains wholly responsible for the dead, he remains their commander. Guilt preserves a role inside the catastrophe.

Home requires a smaller self.

Not an innocent self. Not a powerless self. A participant.

The distinction is easy to state and hard to inhabit. ``It was chance'' can conceal what I did. ``It was all my consequence'' can conceal what I could never control. Between them lies a more granular responsibility: what did I know, what did I choose, what did I conceal, what did others choose, what conditions constrained us, what followed, and what part of that aftermath may honestly be called mine?

The \Od{} never reduces those questions to one formula. That is its strength. It gives us warnings that sometimes save and sometimes merely accuse; leaders who command and are overruled; gods whose action overlaps human decision; punishments that become new injuries; objects that carry old actions forward; stories that rearrange blame; and a final peace that requires the past to stop issuing commands.

Athena's binary does not solve this world. It opens it.

Chance is not simply the opposite of cause. Consequence is not the same as guilt. Responsibility does not require sovereignty.

The sea does not have to belong to Odysseus for his wake to be real.

And his wake is not the sea.

\section*{Note on sources and film dialogue}

Primary Homeric references use conventional book-and-line numbering. The Greek text and A. T. Murray's public-domain translation were checked against the Perseus Digital Library edition. The Nolan dialogue is cited from multiple convergent English subtitle transcriptions of the released 2026 film and checked against a publicly circulating screenplay manuscript. Because the screenplay manuscript's official publication status is unclear, the paper does not treat it as a formally published screenplay edition. Secondary claims are limited to works listed below; where the paper introduces language such as ``causal narcissism,'' ``action space,'' or ``responsibility without sovereignty,'' those phrases are the author's analytic formulations, not ancient terminology.

